.archon/logs/iter-002/plan.jsonl:102:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"stochastic|quadratic|Doob|Brownian|martingale|càdlàg|cadlag\" Manuscript/proof_outline.tex blueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex README.md'","aggregated_output":"README.md:2:# Lean formalization of Brownian motion and stochastic integrals\nREADME.md:4:This is an open, collaborative project with the goal of formalizing stochastic processes and stochastic integration in Lean.\nREADME.md:7:* A formalization of Brownian motion, which is now complete. That task needed a development of the theory of Gaussian distributions, the construction of a projective family of Gaussian distributions and its projective limit by the Kolmogorov extension theorem, and a proof of a Kolmogorov-Chentsov continuity theorem.\nREADME.md:9:* A definition of stochastic integrals and proof of Ito's lemma. This is in progress.\nREADME.md:14:### Brownian motion\nREADME.md:16:We now have a full implementation of a Brownian motion on the positive real half line, and are in the process of migrating our results to Mathlib.\nREADME.md:19:We also have a preprint, available at [https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.20118](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.20118). To cite our work on Brownian motion, please use the following BibTex entry:\nREADME.md:22:  title={Formalization of Brownian motion in Lean},\nREADME.md:32:We are now building the necessary theory to define the integral against a semi-martingale, with the goal to then prove Ito's lemma.\nREADME.md:33:See this Zulip channel for discussion: [zulip link](https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/509433-Brownian-motion)\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:3:The lecture notes at \\href{https://dec41.user.srcf.net/h/III_L/stochastic_calculus_and_applications/}{this link} as well as chapter 18 of \\cite{kallenberg2021} are good references for this chapter.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:105:Can be extended to a measure on the predictable $\\sigma$-algebra if the process is of class DL and ... (see Metivier, Semimartingales, 13.3).\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:107:Let $X : T \\to \\Omega \\to E$ be a stochastic process such that $X_t$ is integrable for all $t$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:120:\\section{Square integrable martingales}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:124:\\begin{definition}[Square integrable martingales]\\label{def:IsSquareIntegrable}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:129:We say that a martingale $M : T \\to \\Omega \\to E$ is square integrable if it is càdlàg and $\\sup_{t \\in T} \\Vert M_t \\Vert_{L^2} < \\infty$ (Lean remark: use \\texttt{eLpNorm (M t) 2}).\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:136:If $M$ and $N$ are square integrable martingales and $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $M + N$ and $a M$ are square integrable martingales.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:143:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:IsSquareIntegrable.submartingale_sq}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:144:  \\uses{def:IsSquareIntegrable, def:Submartingale}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:146:  \\lean{ProbabilityTheory.IsSquareIntegrable.submartingale_sq_norm}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:147:If $M$ is a square integrable martingale, then $\\Vert M \\Vert^2$ is a submartingale.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:151:  \\uses{lem:Martingale.submartingale_convex_comp}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:153:Apply Lemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.submartingale_convex_comp} with the convex function $f: x \\mapsto \\Vert x \\Vert^2$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:161:For $M$ a square integrable martingale, the function $t \\mapsto \\Vert M_t \\Vert_{L^2}$ is non-decreasing.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:165:  \\uses{lem:IsSquareIntegrable.submartingale_sq}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:167:By Lemma~\\ref{lem:IsSquareIntegrable.submartingale_sq}, $\\Vert M_t \\Vert^2$ is a submartingale.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:184:For $M$ a square integrable martingale, we have $M_t \\to M_\\infty$ almost surely and in $L^2$ as $t \\to \\infty$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:188:  \\uses{thm:tendsto_limitProcess_of_cadlag}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:189:TODO: use a martingale convergence theorem. Check whether Theorem~\\ref{thm:tendsto_limitProcess_of_cadlag} is what we need.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:195:For $M$ a square integrable martingale,\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:211:We denote by $\\mathcal{M}^2(E)$ or simply $\\mathcal{M}^2$ the space of equivalence classes with respect to indistinguishability of square integrable martingales $T \\to \\Omega \\to E$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:316:For $V \\in \\mathcal{E}_{T, F}$, $M \\in \\mathcal{M}^2(E)$ and a continuous bilinear map $B: E \\times F \\to G$, the elementary stochastic integral $V \\bullet_B M$ is in $\\mathcal{M}^2(G)$.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:320:  \\uses{lem:cadlag_elemStochIntegralBilin, lem:Martingale.elemStochIntegral, lem:eLpNorm_elemStochIntegralBilin_le}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:321:By Lemma~\\ref{lem:cadlag_elemStochIntegralBilin}, $V \\bullet_B M$ is càdlàg, and we know that it is a martingale by Lemma~\\ref{lem:Martingale.elemStochIntegral}~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:345:\\section{Local martingales}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:350:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:IsLocalMartingale.isLocalSubmartingale_sq_norm}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:351:  \\uses{def:IsLocalMartingale, def:IsLocalSubmartingale}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:353:  \\lean{ProbabilityTheory.IsLocalMartingale.isLocalSubmartingale_sq_norm}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:354:If $M$ is a cadlag local martingale, then $\\Vert M \\Vert^2$ is a cadlag local sub-martingale.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:363:\\begin{definition}[Quadratic variation]\\label{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:364:  \\uses{def:IsLocalMartingale, thm:local_doobMeyer, lem:IsLocalMartingale.isLocalSubmartingale_sq_norm}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:366:  \\lean{ProbabilityTheory.quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:367:For $M$ a local martingale, the predictable quadratic variation of $M$ is defined as the predictable part of the Doob-Meyer decomposition of the local sub-martingale $\\Vert M \\Vert^2$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:372:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:predictable_quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:373:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:374:The quadratic variation $\\langle M \\rangle$ of a local martingale $M$ is a predictable process.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:383:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:cadlag_quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:384:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:385:The quadratic variation $\\langle M \\rangle$ of a local martingale $M$ is cadlag.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:394:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:locallyIntegrable_quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:395:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:396:The quadratic variation $\\langle M \\rangle$ of a local martingale $M$ is locally integrable.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:405:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:quadraticVariation_zero}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:406:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:416:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:monotone_quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:417:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:418:The quadratic variation $\\langle M \\rangle$ of a local martingale $M$ is non-decreasing.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:427:\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:local_martingale_sub_quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:428:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:429:For $M$ a local martingale, the process $\\Vert M_t \\Vert^2 - \\langle M \\rangle_t$ is a local martingale.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:438:\\begin{definition}\\label{def:quadraticVariationMeasure}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:439:  \\uses{def:quadraticVariation, def:StieltjesFunction.measure}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:440:For $M$ a local martingale, we denote by $d\\langle M \\rangle$ the measure on $\\mathbb{R}_+$ associated to the non-decreasing càdlàg function $\\langle M \\rangle$~.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:445:  \\uses{def:IsLocalMartingale, def:quadraticVariation}\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:446:For $M$ and $N$ local martingales, the covariation $\\langle M, N \\rangle$ is a stochastic process defined by polarization of the quadratic variation:\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:451:% For any local martingales $M$ and $N$, there exists a cadlag process $\\langle M,N \\rangle$ with $\\langle M,N \\rangle_0 = 0$ such that $MN - \\langle M,N \\rangle$ is a local martingale. That process is a.s. unique and is called the \\emph{covariation} of $M$ and $N$.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:459:The covariation $\\langle M, N \\rangle$ of local martingales $M$ and $N$ is a predictable process.\nblueprint/src/chapters/stochastic_integral.tex:463:  \\uses{lem:predictable_quadraticVariation}\nblueprin